---
import BaseLayout from "../../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";

// Notes feed: essays and TILs mixed, filterable by tag.
// The matrix post is written in full inline (expandable); others are stubs.
const tags = ["all", "self-hosting", "rust", "graphql", "linux"];
---

<!-- Site C · notes — essays + TILs with working tag filter -->
<BaseLayout
	title="Notes — Tarik Duyar"
	description="Essays and TILs by Tarik Duyar: self-hosting, Rust, GraphQL, Linux."
	bodyClass="bg-[#0e1014] text-gray-300 antialiased"
>
	<main class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-6 py-16 md:py-24">
		<header class="mb-12">
			<div class="flex items-center justify-between mb-10">
				<a href="/13" class="flex items-center gap-3">
					<span class="w-9 h-9 rounded-lg border border-blue-400/30 bg-blue-400/5 flex items-center justify-center">
						<span class="font-oldturcic text-blue-300/80 text-lg leading-none select-none" aria-hidden="true">𐱅</span>
					</span>
					<span class="font-sinistre text-lg text-white">Tarik Duyar</span>
				</a>
				<nav class="flex gap-5 text-sm">
					<a href="/13/notes" class="text-white underline decoration-blue-400/50 underline-offset-4">Notes</a>
					<a href="/13/projects" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-blue-300 transition-colors">Projects</a>
					<a href="/13/now" class="text-gray-400 hover:text-blue-300 transition-colors">Now</a>
				</nav>
			</div>
			<h1 class="font-sinistre text-3xl text-white mb-4">Notes</h1>
			<p class="text-gray-400 leading-relaxed max-w-xl">
				Long essays and two-paragraph TILs, in one stream. Filter by topic.
			</p>

			<!-- Tag filter -->
			<div class="flex flex-wrap gap-2 mt-7" id="tag-bar">
				{tags.map((t) => (
					<button
						type="button"
						data-tag={t}
						class={`tag-btn text-xs px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full border transition-all cursor-pointer ${t === "all" ? "border-blue-400/60 text-blue-300 bg-blue-400/10" : "border-white/10 text-gray-500 hover:border-blue-400/40 hover:text-gray-300"}`}
					>{t}</button>
				))}
			</div>
		</header>

		<!-- Feed -->
		<div class="space-y-5" id="notes-feed">
			<!-- Full essay -->
			<article id="matrix-well-known" class="note rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.015] p-7 scroll-mt-8" data-tags="self-hosting linux">
				<div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-3">
					<span class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-blue-400/70 border border-blue-400/20 rounded px-1.5 py-0.5">essay</span>
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600 tabular-nums">June 2026</span>
				</div>
				<h2 class="font-sinistre text-2xl text-gray-100 mb-3">Self-hosting Matrix: the .well-known rabbit hole</h2>
				<div class="space-y-4 text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
					<p>
						I wanted one thing: a Matrix ID that says <span class="text-blue-300">@tarik:atduyar.com</span>
						instead of advertising whichever subdomain the homeserver lives on.
						This is called delegation, it is allegedly simple, and it took me
						three evenings.
					</p>
					<div class="note-more hidden space-y-4">
						<p>
							Matrix identity is tied to the server name — the part after the
							colon. Set it to your apex domain and every homeserver in the
							federation knocks on atduyar.com:8448 expecting Matrix; your web
							server answers with a confused 404. The fix is publishing a
							.well-known file that says "the real server is over there."
						</p>
						<p>
							<span class="text-gray-200">Evening one</span> went to a
							Content-Type header (the spec wants application/json; my server
							served text/plain). <span class="text-gray-200">Evening two</span>
							went to CORS — the client .well-known is fetched cross-origin by
							browsers, and the error message mentions CORS exactly zero times.
							<span class="text-gray-200">Evening three</span> went to a missing
							:443 in the delegation target, which made federation work for
							exactly half the servers — the half with cached lookups. The most
							misleading failure mode available.
						</p>
						<p>
							The lesson: you don't understand a protocol until you've served it
							wrong three different ways. Was a prettier user ID worth three
							evenings? No. Will I do the same for email next? Yes.
						</p>
					</div>
					<button type="button" class="note-toggle text-blue-400 hover:text-blue-300 transition-colors text-sm cursor-pointer">read the rest ↓</button>
				</div>
				<div class="flex gap-2 mt-5">
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600">#self-hosting</span>
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600">#linux</span>
				</div>
			</article>

			<!-- TIL -->
			<article class="note rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.015] p-7" data-tags="graphql">
				<div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-3">
					<span class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400/70 border border-emerald-400/20 rounded px-1.5 py-0.5">til</span>
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600 tabular-nums">April 2026</span>
				</div>
				<h2 class="text-lg text-gray-100 mb-3">GraphQL persisted queries are free money on TV clients</h2>
				<p class="text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
					TV apps on EgeceTV ship a fixed set of queries, so we swapped full query
					strings for persisted query hashes. Smaller payloads, cacheable GETs at
					the CDN, and the TV clients — the slowest devices we support — felt it
					most. Should have done it a year earlier.
				</p>
				<div class="flex gap-2 mt-5">
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600">#graphql</span>
				</div>
			</article>

			<!-- TIL -->
			<article class="note rounded-xl border border-white/10 bg-white/[0.015] p-7" data-tags="rust">
				<div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-3">
					<span class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-400/70 border border-emerald-400/20 rounded px-1.5 py-0.5">til</span>
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600 tabular-nums">March 2026</span>
				</div>
				<h2 class="text-lg text-gray-100 mb-3">mmap lifetimes don't exist, and the borrow checker knows it</h2>
				<p class="text-sm text-gray-400 leading-relaxed">
					A memory-mapped region's validity depends on the file and the mapping,
					not on anything Rust can see. Working on REKV, my zero-copy reads
					compiled fine right up until they were undefined behavior. The fix was
					an ownership structure where the mapping outlives every borrowed view —
					enforced by types, not comments. Full post brewing.
				</p>
				<div class="flex gap-2 mt-5">
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600">#rust</span>
				</div>
			</article>

			<!-- Draft essay stub -->
			<article class="note rounded-xl border border-white/10 border-dashed bg-transparent p-7" data-tags="graphql">
				<div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-3">
					<span class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-gray-500 border border-white/10 rounded px-1.5 py-0.5">draft</span>
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600">soon</span>
				</div>
				<h2 class="font-sinistre text-2xl text-gray-400 mb-3">Five platforms, one GraphQL API</h2>
				<p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
					The schema decisions that let one backend serve web, mobile, and two TV
					platforms — and the ones I'd take back.
				</p>
				<div class="flex gap-2 mt-5">
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600">#graphql</span>
				</div>
			</article>

			<!-- Draft essay stub -->
			<article class="note rounded-xl border border-white/10 border-dashed bg-transparent p-7" data-tags="linux self-hosting">
				<div class="flex flex-wrap items-baseline justify-between gap-2 mb-3">
					<span class="text-[10px] uppercase tracking-wider text-gray-500 border border-white/10 rounded px-1.5 py-0.5">draft</span>
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600">soon</span>
				</div>
				<h2 class="font-sinistre text-2xl text-gray-400 mb-3">Everything I self-host, and what it actually costs</h2>
				<p class="text-sm text-gray-500 leading-relaxed">
					An honest audit: the services, the VPS bill, the hours, and whether any
					of it is rational. (Spoiler: the hours are the bill.)
				</p>
				<div class="flex gap-2 mt-5">
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600">#linux</span>
					<span class="text-xs text-gray-600">#self-hosting</span>
				</div>
			</article>
		</div>

		<p id="empty-msg" class="hidden text-sm text-gray-600 text-center py-10">Nothing under this tag yet.</p>

		<footer class="mt-16 pt-8 border-t border-white/5 flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 text-xs text-gray-600">
			<a href="/13" class="hover:text-blue-300 transition-colors">← home</a>
			<span class="font-oldturcic text-blue-400/20 tracking-[0.3em] select-none" aria-hidden="true">𐰋𐰃𐰠𐰏</span>
		</footer>
	</main>

	<script is:inline>
		// Tag filtering
		const buttons = document.querySelectorAll(".tag-btn");
		const notes = document.querySelectorAll(".note");
		const emptyMsg = document.getElementById("empty-msg");
		const active = ["border-blue-400/60", "text-blue-300", "bg-blue-400/10"];
		const inactive = ["border-white/10", "text-gray-500"];

		for (const btn of buttons) {
			btn.addEventListener("click", () => {
				for (const b of buttons) {
					b.classList.remove(...active);
					b.classList.add(...inactive);
				}
				btn.classList.add(...active);
				btn.classList.remove(...inactive);

				const tag = btn.dataset.tag;
				let visible = 0;
				for (const note of notes) {
					const show = tag === "all" || note.dataset.tags.split(" ").includes(tag);
					note.classList.toggle("hidden", !show);
					if (show) visible++;
				}
				emptyMsg.classList.toggle("hidden", visible > 0);
			});
		}

		// Expandable essay
		for (const toggle of document.querySelectorAll(".note-toggle")) {
			toggle.addEventListener("click", () => {
				const more = toggle.parentElement.querySelector(".note-more");
				const open = more.classList.toggle("hidden");
				toggle.textContent = open ? "read the rest ↓" : "show less ↑";
			});
		}
	</script>

	<style is:global>
		html { background-color: #0e1014; color-scheme: dark; }
	</style>
</BaseLayout>
